<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Android 11 form of the rules in `data_extraction_rules.xml`, which is
  where the reasoning lives.

  This is the file Android 10 and 11 (API 29 and 30) read, and the only one
  they read. `android:fullBackupContent` is consulted up to Android 11 and
  `android:dataExtractionRules` from Android 12 (API 31) onwards, so a device on
  29 or 30 takes this file and one on 31 or later takes the other and ignores
  this one. `minSdk` is 29, which is what makes both live.

  What it stands between: on 29 and 30 `android:dataExtractionRules` is ignored, and an
  app with no `android:fullBackupContent` gets *unrestricted* full backup: the
  passphrase-less SSH key and the server's connection token included, off the
  device and into the cloud. Nothing would fail, nothing would be logged, and the
  cause would be a manifest edit from months earlier.

  Keep it identical to the `<cloud-backup>` section of
  `data_extraction_rules.xml`; the two disagreeing is the only way this
  arrangement can quietly go wrong, and `BackupRulesParityTest` compares them.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="file" path="home/.vscodroid/data/Machine" />
</full-backup-content>
